  "knowledge": {
    "progressSummary": "COMPLETED: Proved that every closed knight's tour on any n*n board (n >= 5) has >= 4 oblique turns. The proof is purely algebraic, generalizing Knuth's 8x8 result. Key theorem: four_oblique_corners exhibits 4 distinct positions (the corners) where turns are oblique because the dot product of entry/exit vectors is always -4.",
    "builtItems": [
      "Created KnightsTourObliqueOQ01.lean: parameterized board, knight graph, corner analysis, main theorem",
      "Proved cornerXX_neighbors: degree-2 theorems for all 4 corners (algebraic elimination of 6/8 offsets)",
      "Proved corner_forces_oblique: universal oblique turn theorem at any corner (dot = -4)",
      "Proved four_oblique_corners: 4 distinct oblique positions exist in any closed tour on n*n (n >= 5)",
      "Created gallery entry with meta.json, annotations.json, index.ts"
    ],
    "insights": [
      "For n >= 5, each corner of the n*n board has exactly 2 knight-adjacent squares (6 of 8 offsets produce negative coordinates, impossible in Fin n)",
      "The dot product of entry/exit move vectors at any corner is always -4, independent of board size: (-offset1) . offset2 = -(1*2 + 2*1) = -4",
      "The corner-forcing mechanism is purely algebraic — no native_decide or enumeration needed, unlike the 8x8 parent proof",
      "The OQ01 source proves a universal lower bound for every closed tour; the 8x8 example shows this lower bound cannot be improved uniformly."
    ],
    "mathlibGaps": [],
    "nextSteps": [
      "Does the minimum oblique count grow with n? Investigate whether boards with n >> 8 have minimum > 4",
      "Can edge squares (degree 3-4) also force oblique turns under certain conditions?"
    ],
